      "provenance": "literature_retrieved",
      "rationale": "[Literature audit 2026-11-05 (deliverome-analysis PR 236), verdict region_only: region-level site confirmed; no junction recoverable. validation_level surface_and_function -> surface_only (paper reports surface: yes, function: not_reported). Paper (PMID 38825009): \"We created a FLAG-tagged full-length human CLCA4 (22-917) by inserting a FLAG sequence (DYKDDDDK) between the predicted FnIII and GPI anchor domains (CLCA4-FLAG) (Fig. 1A). ... Domain boundaries were predicted by means of the Phyre2 Protein Fold Recognition Server.\". Audit note: Not recoverable from the text: the boundaries are Phyre2 predictions and no residue number is given (Fig 1A may carry numbers but the PMC figure was not retrievable here). Residues 1-21 are replaced by the pHLsec vector signal peptide. Surface/GPI anchorage: C-CLCA4-FLAG is cell-associated and released into the medium by PI-PLC applied to intact HEK293T cells on ice (Fig 1E), which is a surface readout without antibody staining. Function (I_CaCC potentiation) was assayed with untagged or VWA constructs, not shown for CLCA4-FLAG; the row's surface_and_function overstates it.] The paper explicitly places a FLAG tag between the FnIII and GPI anchor domains of full-length, membrane-anchored CLCA4 and validates surface display by cell-pellet fractionation and PI-PLC release. No specific residue number is given in the ledger text, so insert_after_residue is left null and the named inter-domain region is recorded. The entire span 22\u2013919 is extracellular per the topology landmarks, so any junction in this region is on the cell surface. Ranked first because surface localisation and GPI anchor function are both experimentally confirmed. Full-length human CLCA4 (residues 22\u2013917) with a FLAG (DYKDDDDK) sequence inserted between the predicted FnIII and GPI anchor domains (CLCA4-FLAG), expressed in mammalian cells. L2 confirms the C-terminal FLAG-tagged fragment (C-CLCA4-FLAG) is cell-associated (detected only in cell pellets, not media). L3 confirms PI-PLC treatment releases C-CLCA4-FLAG into medium, demonstrating GPI anchorage and surface localisation.",
